IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc9-support.l4 -- three-valued helpers used by the ccc9 modules (Criminal Code, ss 270 to 278.31).
-- THESE FUNCTIONS IMPLEMENT NO PROVISION. They combine answers of type MAYBE BOOLEAN (JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE, NOTHING) the way the
-- rule of 2026-10-29 needs: a case the text says no to stays a no, and a case the encoding cannot answer ("not covered", NOTHING)
-- stays NOTHING and is never turned into FALSE. Their names carry the label of the first section of this part, `s 270`, only so that
-- no other part can define the same name.
--   every one of these holds:    JUST FALSE if any answer is JUST FALSE; else NOTHING if any answer is NOTHING; else JUST TRUE
--   at least one of these holds: JUST TRUE if any answer is JUST TRUE; else NOTHING if any answer is NOTHING; else JUST FALSE
--   the opposite of:             JUST TRUE <-> JUST FALSE, NOTHING stays NOTHING
-- They quote no text, so none of their branches carries a FALSE-kind or NOTHING-kind quotation; each is tagged HELPER instead.

GIVEN answers IS A LIST OF MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 270 helper: every one of these holds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF any (GIVEN a YIELD a EQUALS JUST FALSE) answers THEN JUST FALSE   -- HELPER (carries an input's no; quotes no text)
        IF any (GIVEN a YIELD a EQUALS NOTHING) answers THEN NOTHING   -- HELPER (carries an input's not-covered; quotes no text)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

GIVEN answers IS A LIST OF MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 270 helper: at least one of these holds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF any (GIVEN a YIELD a EQUALS JUST TRUE) answers THEN JUST TRUE
        IF any (GIVEN a YIELD a EQUALS NOTHING) answers THEN NOTHING   -- HELPER (carries an input's not-covered; quotes no text)
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- HELPER (every input is a no; quotes no text)

GIVEN answer IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 270 helper: the opposite of` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- HELPER (carries an input's not-covered; quotes no text)
    WHEN JUST b THEN JUST (NOT b)

-- The date arithmetic that "at least N days before" and "N years have elapsed" need, written once.
-- A day count is the difference of the two dates' day numbers (daydate `Day`); the first day and the last day are both counted as the
-- difference counts them (so 1 January to 3 January is 2 days). The Interpretation Act's rules for "clear days" are not among the
-- sources: fork F-days in NOTES.md.
GIVEN earlier IS A DATE, later IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 270 helper: the days from the earlier date to the later date` MEANS
    (Day later) MINUS (Day earlier)
